@@@@ open-pigeonhole-principle
situation: open
topic: The pigeonhole principle
voice: plain
level: medium
expect: {"askMustNot":["\\b9\\b","nine","2 (×|x|\\*|times) 4"],"must":["hash"]}
noverify: an opening; it asks for the smallest number of draws (9) and does not state it
@@ reply
**Why this matters**

The pigeonhole principle is the counting argument behind every proof that a collision must exist. It is why any hash function with a fixed output size has two inputs with the same digest, why no lossless compressor can shrink every file, and why some server in a pool always carries at least the average load. It proves that something exists without finding it, which is the usual shape of a lower bound in algorithms. It also leads into counting arguments used in probability and in the analysis of hash tables.

<ask type="short">A drawer holds loose socks in 4 colours, plenty of each. What is the smallest number you must take out, without looking, to be certain that 3 of them share a colour? Say how you know that number is enough and that one fewer is not.</ask>

@@@@ open-quiz-modular-arithmetic
situation: open
